\section{Data used}{

Target-disease associations are pooled from up to three results tables
(\code{sources}), restricted to the targets of \code{network_edges} in scope:
\itemize{
\item \code{"profile"} -- \code{\link[=targets_disease_profile]{targets_disease_profile()}} (Open Targets, each target's
top diseases with their resolved names; the main source).
\item \code{"disease_genes"} -- \code{\link[=disease_genes_fetch]{disease_genes_fetch()}}'s custom disease gene sets
(e.g. hypertension \code{MONDO_0005044}, inflammation \code{GO_0006954}); a target
in the set counts as associated (GO gene sets carry no score).
\item \code{"targets_disease"} -- \code{\link[=targets_disease_filter]{targets_disease_filter()}}'s per-target score for
one chosen disease.
Rows with an association score below \code{min_score} are dropped (rows
without a score are kept); a target-disease pair found in several sources
is counted once, with its highest score. Disease names come from
\code{targets_disease_profile} or \code{disease_genes} (the raw ID when neither
names it).
}
}

\section{Focus diseases}{

The diseases of \code{disease_genes} and \code{targets_disease} are the ones the
analysis was designed around, so by default (\code{focus = NULL}) they are the
"focus" diseases: always shown in the \code{"class"}/\code{"disease"} views
(whatever their rank), drawn as squares with a bold label, and given their
own columns in the heatmap. They also count towards their area (the
hypertension gene set is part of "Cardiovascular"). Pass a character
vector of \code{disease_id}s to choose them, or \code{character(0)} for none.
}

\section{Disease areas are keyword rules, on purpose}{

No disease ontology is available offline, so a disease is assigned to the
first area (in list order) with a pattern matching its name
(case-insensitive Perl regular expressions); unmatched names go to
\code{"Other"}. The rules are plain data -- print \code{\link[=disease_map_classes]{disease_map_classes()}} to
read them, and pass a modified copy as \code{disease_classes}, e.g.
\code{modifyList(disease_map_classes(), list(Skin = c("skin", "derma")))}.
The classification table returned as \code{attr(result, "classification")}
lists every target-disease pair with its area, so any assignment can be
checked.
}

\section{Which targets are drawn}{

Only proteins associated with at least one disease shown on the right are
candidates. With \code{min_shared = NULL} the \code{max_targets} candidates hit by
the most compounds are drawn (ties: more disease links, then higher mean
prediction probability); with \code{min_shared = k} every candidate hit by at
least \code{k} compounds is. Compounds none of whose targets is drawn are
listed at the bottom of the left column, without edges. The counts on the
right (targets, diseases) always refer to every target in scope.
}

